      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/46022590cec5995ebae5bc7f4883750027815316173d7086ce34ca776ac72e80.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/b3112e3660499f16d64376204a5912df73de570bfffa3f6c9b5c8c25b439930f.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs II, III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2211c0b1-37da-5adb-b064-91a2a06c2af3",
