      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/cbaee2e7446b2f5a42beed0324cdf9543a8d5a2641a1ed65b7d8ce2a4689d93f.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/67f3bda93577cb9425168c505aeefacb003bf2c5d3a1b2bcd015fc2f698e2523.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b0fc6c6e-eafa-529c-afa7-429f79c41d79",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
