      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/095b39a84ebaffaee4ab8f8ca7d0db3137faf2447dd041f9bb141e4be7264d87.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/10161afc6f94e86d3845cd9f7593d7db80fab66a5319bf5c813be17d4661eadd.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f5d21686-6990-566e-963c-f1a1d56fcf9e",
