      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/4c34402cb1526f8c1a54ccfc0410a7ca60a4d1044669747d6605a8e53ef265e6.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/7f3d140332c92e37d73b36482169d4e8abbbca9d93005c5f6e35e5730984e6f4.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph I, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs II, III, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "85bbc6cd-a822-5789-8d0a-965ecaba9de6",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
