      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/e9a602d1d87a4f1dd3fbe7b90e7e3ba41049489ef064733e8820ba229809147c.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/a6bad0269dab1927b3948eb98d0e30df5af1c6930304f8e15f3f2a8d739e3624.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs II, IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c6949b09-264c-50b7-9f26-a722b1f181ed",
