    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6a80cbf3-d0be-5490-a53d-5a894b4b2b87",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/a89459a101f58ee34f2a23fb9e01029d1e1f39ee17c911999ed7c8214fbe9b72.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nEach graph shows its entire domain; an open circle excludes its endpoint.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/796d17fecf02fd309fb8f9ede53a111af50e34a561723a8b9f97dda2cd04140d.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
